Z||000000||Ed||02-26-2000||01:03 PM||||A couple of days ago I posted a question on my power window. The window goes all the way down. When switched up it comes up about two to three inches and stops. <P>When jiggled it will continue up.<P>I can hear the window motor whirring around in there so I'm sure I'm getting power to the motor. <P>Do the limiters get loose or out of adjustment?  I'm about to take off the door panel and look around, but I'm giving the experts here another chance to diagnose this problem from afar. <P>Any suggestions? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||02-26-2000||01:18 PM||||if the motor is spinning but not lifting it would seem the motor is clutched inside.<P>either the clucth is fried or the window is binding in the rails.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ed||02-26-2000||01:46 PM||||The window binding in the rails sounds like it, especially since jiggling frees it up. I'll go in there and bend it back into shape. <BR>(I hope!) Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ed||02-26-2000||04:23 PM||||Went into the door and looked at the window mechanicals. I think something isn't rotating that should be. Probably a flat spot on a roller.  The next step would be to pull off the metal liner and look closer. Low chance of success based on my mechanical abilities. <P>I can jiggle the window or the switch and make it work, so that's it for now. Next step would be a professional mechanic, only if it gets worse. <P>Thanks to all for advice. ||||reg||9
Z||000004||Luperman||02-26-2000||10:55 PM||||hey Ed I've had alot of experience with power windows and the main thing you might want to check is your switch you said you can jiggle it and the window will move again well that could be because the connector in back is loose connection is dirty if that does'nt fix either take the switch apart and clean (but attention to how it goes back together again) or replace the switch, the switch are subjected to alot of abuse for rain water road dirt it all finds it's way in there , I can bet you all it needs is a good cleaning on the inside of the switch ,because you said it would roll down no problem gravity less resistance in the switch<BR>when you roll it up more of a power drain if the switch was dirty,(sorry the elactrical terminolgy) hope this gives you a new direction to look in before seeing some witch doctorwho will just throw some chicken bones.<p>[This message has been edited by Luperman (edited 02-26-2000).]||||reg||6
Z||000005||Ed||02-26-2000||11:54 PM||||Yours is the second suggestion to work on the switch. But the reason I haven't is that I can hear the window motor clicking when the switch is pressed. I haven't put a light on the connection at the window motor, but I think that power is getting to the motor.  <P>The whole exercise reminds me of rocking the car to get it out of mud. Rocking the switch will move the window and rocking the window will move the window.  Plus it works some of the time anyway.  <P>The window stops dead at the same place when it fails, leading me to think its a mechanical problem instead of electrical, unless there's a flat spot in the motor windings which will force me to get a new motor.  ||||reg||1
